s from cities  all over the world um  sampled different times across different  years and yet we still have this core  set of species  and what is striking about that core set  of species when you break them down into  their groups is that they're one clearly  phylogenetically organized they come  from three different phyla accident  bacteria from acuitys and proteobacteria  notably you don't see the bacteroidetes  in this there's there's no core  micromethod bacteroidetes notably lc you  don't see any viruses or fungi  that show up in this corbin microbond  there are some in the sub core uh  sacramento bca is of course in the is  the core that's pretty common it just  doesn't quite make the cutoff for a core  microbe  um but these are phytogenically  organized in the three phyla  um and what's  possibly the most interesting feature  here in my opinion is that  the majority of these taxes the majority  of these core taxes are found in 95 of  samples are not human commensal taxa  which is weird because the only thing  that you can really say about cities  definitively  uh the only thing that's common across  all cities everywhere in the world that  there are no exceptions to is that  humans live in these cities so the only  thing that we really expect to be  driving the similarity between cities is  the fact that there are humans in these  cities and we would expect human  commensals to maybe found every sample  but we find that the majority of our  core microbes are not typically  considered human conventional species  not to say they're never found in humans  just so they're not typically considered  human commensal species which gives us  this kind of striking pattern that we  can't quite explain why this is the case  yet but gives us a striking pattern  of a non-human ecosystem that we believe  is interacting and probably viable  and performing interesting functions in  the urban space  now  the fact that this core microbiome  exists is not to distract from the fact  that there are indeed continental  differences between different samples  so we have a  fairly large continental divergence that  you can see when we plot all of these  samples out of umap all of the taxonomic  profiles for the samples on the umap  and net tax and that geographic  difference largely separates east asian  samples of samples primarily from hong  kong japan  um  korea taiwan  um largely separates these samples from  samples taken from europe and north  america  um and we see a fairly big continent but  we also see smaller clusters that  correspond to sub-saharan africa to uh  to oceania to the middle east and these  clusters are mainly smaller modus things  only because we don't have as many  samples from this region our expectation  is that we add samples from this region  these clusters will become larger and  we'll start to see more clear  continental divergence between all of  these regions  and that speaks to maybe some  interesting identifiable trends and  that's a concept that when we were  researching this when we did this  research we wanted to push a little bit  farther  because what we found is not just that  there is a core  urban microbiome  that is conserved across a large subset  of samples  but that there also appear to be  certain signatures that encode certain  properties of the cities from where a  microbe is taken  that are also  conserved across the world and what i  mean by that uh specifically is that we  can find a certain set of microbes that  will reliably indicate  whether a sample was taken from a city  on the coast  from a city that has high population  from a city that has a warm climate or  cold climate  and how that works basically is we  trained a random forest model to predict  uh a city's population climate whatever  you you would like really whatever you  can see on this plot here we trained a  random forest model to predict this from  uh all of our data except for the data  from one city and then we asked the  model to predict  the uh labels for the held out city we  asked to predict the climate the  population  for samples in the held out city and we  were able to do far far better than  random when we did something like that  and what that tells us is there's some  kind of geographically con sorry  globally conserved signature that  indicates these features of a city  which we find to be really surprising  really interesting  and completely unexplainable at this  point  because  when we looked into some of these some  of these are sort of abstract concepts  it's hard to say what microbes might be  associated with population for example  when we looked into this data  we thought that we would be able to  explain  some of these features really well in  particular we thought that we would be  able to explain what microbes were  associated with coastal cities really  really easily because we thought they  were going to be halophilic microbes we  thought they were they were going to be  ocean loving microbes we thought that  they were going to be stuff that's found  in water normally makes sense that would  be associated with the coast  it wasn't what we found though not at  all um what we found is that of the sort  of top 20 most associated microbes with  coastal cities  they were not  except for two of them associated with  water at all and any documented evidence  that we could find so at this point our  ability to explain why these signatures  exist why they contain the species they  contain is very very limited and this is  one of the main ongoing areas of  research here is understanding why these  signatures exist and how they have a  mechanistic effect  on their environment and how the  environment mechanistically affects them  and with that kind of unknown sense i  want to then touch on the the most  important piece of unknown data here  which is the fact that  most of the dna in these samples can't  even be identified so the red bars here  represent sequences that are aligned to  anything in a genome at 80 average  nucleotide identity across all of our  samples  so  what we see is that almost 50 of our  data  cannot be aligned to any sequence in a  known reference database at 80 average  nucleotide identity and you know for the  the other bio partitions out there  that's using blast should be able to  pick it up with that that percent  identity  and what that tells us is that  there is a great deal of unknown dna  that comes from likely unknown or poorly  characterized clades with  new and novel genes that are out there  in these urban microbiomes that are  floating around us every single day but  that we know very very little about  and some of that surprisingly even comes  from fairly well characterized regions  like europe and north america that we  might expect to have less often still  have a very very high proportion of  unknown dna  and now  i want to  sort of switch gears a little bit  because what i've been talking about the  research we've been doing on urban  microbes uh for the past five years was  until pretty recently  considered maybe a little bit abstract  possibly a little bit academic the idea  was that we were studying urban microbes  we wanted to understand how they  impacted human health  but most of our research was centered on  the idea that we might be tracking  antimicrobial resistance we might be  tracking kind of subclinical effects we  might be able to explain a little bit  about urban specific diseases  but that a lot of these endpoints a lot  of this research would be  kind of a ways down the line that we  wouldn't be able to right away  understand what was happening  in some kind of disease system just  because we felt that these major disease  systems were uh not too common not to  present anymore  and then of course there was the uh the  coven 19 pandemic  and this really changed the way we were  looking at all of these urban  microbiomes because it sort of radically  altered this notion of microbial  surveillance particularly for the  purposes of public health  and we were fortunate enough to actually  take a bunch of samples  during the pandemic that let us get a  give a  idea get a sense of what would happen to  these urban microbiomes and this is this  unprecedented social change so  what we saw there um  is this sort of  surprising rapid and sustained drop  in microbial diversity uh throughout the  pandemic that we feel is probably  largely driven by people not entering  public spaces as much so what you can  see here is a plot of the microbial  diversity the richness the number of  species  uh in the two busiest stations in new  york city the two busiest subway  stations in new york city over the two  weeks leading up to the initial march  shutdowns in 2020 and what we see in  that time is a pretty steep drop off in  the species richness  during those stations in that time  and if we look at this same trend over  many different cities over a much larger  time period we see  just the same drop we see this big  continuous drop over a fairly long time  period that's happening all over the  world  and this drop is  corresponding with a drop in the  fraction of human rna that we're seeing  in these samples so that's just actual  human shed that we see in these samples  and the uh the  number of human associated microbes that  are in the sample so we see these  big traditions riches drop-offs that we  believe is probably driven by a decrease  in humans a decrease in humans  interacting with public spaces  and  this notion of being able to  use molecular surveillance to understand  something about the disease system is  really what  has then motivated a lot of the work  that bioshock has done in the work that  i'm now doing at bioshock  because we took this idea  of microbial surveillance what you could  do with it and we wanted to apply it to  the idea of detecting variants in star  scope too of course variants have gotten  a lot of attention lately with 