of course we were we were happy to  see that as well as a lot of research  use only endpoints that we add to it to  assess lineage to assess variance  as well  basically the idea of all this is once  we have our  um stars go to dna that's that's been  enriched rna excuse me  so let's go through rna  from  a sample we can sequence it we can align  it to the stars co2 genome we can pull  out a bunch of different research  endpoints from that  in particular things like the variants  that are found in the genome all the  little indel snips any any kind of  difference between the woohound  reference you know we can find our data  we can list out  we can identify easily for clinicians  for patients for public health  departments what kind of genes those  variants can occur in  and connect them with any clinical  literature that we have available that  shows if a particular variant is  associated with  any certain type of increase in  virulence  increase in infectivity increase in  danger to people  and then we can also place samples  within a lineage  showing where the other similar clades  have been geographically what kind of  emerging clades are there of course now  we're mostly thinking about delta and  how we can identify delta and the  substance of delta in this  and this ability to  place and identify variants has proved  surprisingly important  and persian because it's actually helped  us to identify novel variants already in  new york city so we took as part of the  validation of this sample we took 71  nasopharyngeal swabs taken from people  in new york city  and we were able to detect a set of  conserved mutations in these samples  that were not already known in gsaid and  of course then the nice thing is that we  can automatically put those samples back  up in gsa so they're accessible and  useful to the broader research community  in general and this has allowed us to  better  identify novel genetic variants and  identify  potential new strains  well very potentially new strains at  this point  but to give this information really to  public health officials to clinicians to  any researcher that's interested in this  kind of thing and make it easy for them  to assess where their samples are  what kind of new variants they need to  look out for and what sort of the trend  in their area might be  and  with that i just want to reiterate  what we've done  is build a molecular assay that allows  uh high brain strainotyping and variant  detection in starscope 2.  this has been built by a really  fantastic team here at bioshock of which  i'm lucky to not be a part of and of  course it's been built in partnership  with a lot of different people and  particularly twist bioscience who did  the the wet lab hybrid capture component  of this assay  and with that i'm going to end the talk  here you can enter questions in the in  the chat box here and i will answer them  as we get them thank you very much for  listening to this talk
 

